text:
Maria G: Heads up — hitting a weird one in the onboarding sample app on Android 12. When using a system proxy + OkHttp 4.11, our token-refresh path sometimes drops the Authorization header on the retry. Seen it twice in smoke tests.

Jonathan K: Hmm. Is this with an authenticator or an interceptor doing the refresh? Any short repro?

Maria G: Authenticator (okhttp.Authenticator) + an application interceptor that adds the header. Short repro: call protected endpoint, get 401, authenticator makes sync refresh request (Retrofit), returns new token. Original request is retried but the Authorization header is missing. Only happens behind our corporate proxy.

Alex P: @Maria G can you paste the client config? Also which thread/executor does the authenticator run on (sync vs coroutine blocking)?

Maria G: Config follows:

```java
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .proxy(ProxySelector.getDefault())
  .addInterceptor(new AuthHeaderInterceptor()) // application interceptor
  .authenticator(new TokenRefreshAuthenticator())
  .build();

// Authenticator does a synchronous Retrofit call:
Response<Token> r = tokenService.refresh(...).execute();
```

Maria G: Repro steps: 1) Enable corporate proxy on emulator 2) Start sample app with an expired token 3) Trigger call to /v1/user 4) Observe 401 -> refresh -> retried request missing header

Jonathan K: Two ideas: (1) application interceptors are not re-run on redirects/authenticator retries? I thought they were, but network interceptors are different. (2) Proxy may cause a 407 flow that converts the request before retry. Can you check whether the retried request is a different instance (headers stripped) or if our interceptor failed to reattach?

Maria G: I captured verbose logs. When the authenticator returns a request via:

```java
return originalRequest.newBuilder()
  .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + newToken)
  .build();
```

It looks like the header is there in the Request object inside OkHttp, but the bytes over the wire show no Authorization header to the upstream service. The proxy adds Proxy-Authorization (407) sometimes.

Alex P: That hints at proxy interaction. OkHttp treats proxy auth as a hop-by-hop header and might remove Authorization when sending to proxy vs origin. Also if you rebuilt the request but didn't set .method(body) correctly for bodies like POST, some servers/proxies can change behavior.

Jonathan K: Another angle: are you using ConnectionSpec/TLS that forces an HTTP/1.1 downgrade? Proxy + NTLM/Negotiate can trigger handshake flows that mutate headers. Can you try forcing direct connection (no proxy) to confirm repro only behind proxy?

Maria G: Confirmed: with proxy disabled, retry keeps Authorization and everything works. With proxy enabled, lost.

Alex P: OK. For a short mitigation, move header injection to a network interceptor. Network interceptors run later (on the wire) and will definitely reapply on retries at network level. But be careful: network interceptors see the connection; they run after proxy auth handling.

Maria G: Tried network interceptor -> works in the repro. But that breaks a bunch of tests that assert header exists earlier in the chain (we rely on app-level header for some metrics).

Jonathan K: If you need both, you can keep app interceptor for in-process consumers and add a small network interceptor that ensures Authorization exists before the request goes out: if header missing, copy from token store. Something like:

```java
@Override public Response intercept(Chain chain) throws IOException {
  Request req = chain.request();
  if (req.header("Authorization") == null) {
    String t = TokenStore.get();
    req = req.newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer " + t).build();
  }
  return chain.proceed(req);
}
```

Alex P: Also add metrics/logging to detect this case. Could be a bug in OkHttp when a proxy transforms the request and clears certain headers. Worth filing upstream if you can make a tiny Java-only repro (no Retrofit) that uses ProxySelector + Authenticator and prints request headers pre/post wire.

Maria G: Will try that. For the SDK, should we update our sample app docs to recommend network interceptor for token header in proxy environments, and add a note about authenticator refresh running synchronously (blocking) and causing potential deadlocks if you use same client instance?

Jonathan K: Yes. Also call out Android emulator proxy flakiness and suggest a direct-forwarding proxy (mitmproxy) for reliable repro.

sdk-bot: [integration test run] devex/sample-app (android) — smoke-tests — FAILED — 1 flaky test detected :face_with_rolling_eyes:

Maria G: thx all, I'll open a short PR to update docs + add the network-interceptor safeguard, and followup on an upstream issue if I can isolate a tiny repro.

Alex P: SG, ping me on the PR and I'll review. :thumbsup:
